NetPi LAME 4.0 maintenance fork
==============================

This repository starts with the unmodified LAME 4.0 release. It preserves the
upstream API, version string and licenses. The NetPi changes are maintained in
a separate pull request; this is not an official LAME release.

Source: https://deb.debian.org/debian/pool/main/l/lame/lame_4.0.orig.tar.gz
SHA-256: 3df5124d5ad3a98312ffd7ba6a9b36230e4f8a3e66d3ce0f425e336c32d216eb
Read COPYING and LICENSE for upstream licensing. Upstream notices remain intact.

Changes
-------

* Preserve per-band minimum scalefactors in VBR out-of-bits recovery (#490).
* Disable the incompatible noise-shaping amplification in CBR/ABR (#516).
* Save the selected quantization candidate's bit count in the older loops.
* Honor --disable-ieeehack and the numeric value of its configuration macro.
* Check allocation failures, unwind partial initialization, and support retry.
* Allocate the internal ReplayGain state only when it is requested.
* Resample whenever input/output rates differ, including differences of 1 Hz.
* Use unsigned bit operations where sanitizers found signed-shift undefined behavior.
* Offer opt-in AArch64 NEON Huffman counting and explicit fast-log selection.
* Build with recorded source/toolchain inputs, optional Cortex-A53/LTO/PGO
  profiles, immutable library paths, and an atomic active-library symlink.

Relevant upstream reports and implementation:
https://sourceforge.net/p/lame/bugs/490/
https://sourceforge.net/p/lame/bugs/516/
https://sourceforge.net/p/lame/patches/101/
https://svn.code.sf.net/p/lame/svn/trunk/lame/doc/html/history.html
The NEON file preserves its upstream r7051 attribution and license notice.

Build for NetPi
---------------

Dependencies: GCC, make, Python >= 3.11, FFmpeg, standard GNU install/coreutils.
Build and install locally on the target; the installer executes the new library.

  python3 scripts/build-netpi.py
  python3 scripts/build-netpi.py --check
  python3 scripts/build-netpi.py --force

Default prefix: /opt/netpi/lame-4.0. Building runs as the invoking user; sudo is
used only for installation if that prefix is not writable. --prefix can select
an absolute writable location outside the source tree. It cannot contain spaces
or colons because the dynamic loader interprets those as LD_PRELOAD separators.

The active lib/libmp3lame.so.0 is a symlink to builds/<input-id>-<binary-sha256>/.
Each build retains manifest.json, config.log and configure/build output. A
changed source tree, compiler, flags, profile or training corpus rebuilds it.
--force rebuilds even when those inputs match. Existing build files remain in
place so already-admitted jobs can use their resolved paths during an upgrade.
Do not overwrite files inside builds/ manually. The system LAME is untouched.

Experimental profiles (compare one change at a time on the actual Pi):

  python3 scripts/build-netpi.py --profile cortex-a53
  python3 scripts/build-netpi.py --lto
  python3 scripts/build-netpi.py --neon
  python3 scripts/build-netpi.py --fast-log yes
  python3 scripts/build-netpi.py --fp-model strict
  python3 scripts/build-netpi.py --pgo-corpus /path/to/representative-lossless-music

The Cortex-A53 profile requires an AArch64 compiler and native execution. NEON
is off by default; enabling it on unsupported architectures fails explicitly.
PGO trains both mono and stereo V0 on WAV/FLAC files, then rebuilds in the same
build directory with profiles outside make-clean directories. Only use music
you are entitled to use; training audio is not uploaded or installed. The
manifest records relative corpus names and hashes. LTO/PGO target GCC.

The generic build explicitly preserves upstream's -O3 and fast-math defaults.
CPPFLAGS/CFLAGS/LDFLAGS and toolchain settings are recorded. Profile and floating
point options are appended after user CFLAGS so the named profile is accurate.
--ieee-hack retains the historical path for comparisons. The corrected default
and CBR/ABR fixes may change encoded bytes; no listening-quality gain is claimed.

Validation
----------

  mkdir /tmp/lame-test-build
  cd /tmp/lame-test-build
  /path/to/lame/configure --disable-frontend --disable-decoder --disable-nasm \
    --enable-shared --enable-static --disable-neon --disable-ieeehack
  make -j2
  bash /path/to/lame/tests/netpi/run-tests.sh /tmp/lame-test-build

The suite injects allocation failures, checks cleanup and retry output, verifies
resampling duration, tests VBR/CBR/ABR over mono/stereo and multiple rates, checks
VBR scalefactor bounds, and compares NEON/scalar integer bit counts. CI also
decodes generated MP3s, runs ASan/UBSan, executes AArch64 builds under QEMU and
compares complete scalar/NEON streams. tests/netpi/test_installer.py exercises
real installation, reuse, force rebuilding, changed flags, LTO and PGO.

QEMU validates execution and output; it does not measure Raspberry Pi speed.
Native Pi timing, thermal behavior and level-matched ABX listening remain target
validation tasks. No measured ARM speedup or audible improvement is asserted.
Compare CPU time, total conversion time, RSS and temperature with the same music.
Quantization, fast-log and floating-point changes need listening checks; byte
differences alone neither demonstrate improvement nor demonstrate degradation.

Scope
-----

NetPi uses FFmpeg planar PCM, V0 and separate rsgain analysis. Its build/loader
changes are a companion PR in lemmsar/netpi. That PR vendors a checksum-verified
patch against the public release so devices need no access token for this fork.

V0 and Joint Stereo presets are unchanged. No speculative psychoacoustic tuning
or frame-level parallelization is introduced. For batch throughput, compare
NetPi's existing worker setting at 1 and 2 under actual playback/thermal load.
Global quantization tables still have legacy initialization shared within a
process. This fork does not promise thread-safe concurrent instance setup;
NetPi's per-file FFmpeg processes do not share that state. Replacing all those
tables or rewriting FFT kernels requires separate profiling and numeric review.
